{"name":"Banana Bandanna","hex":"#f8f739","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"banana","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The elongated curved tropical fruit of a banana plant, which grows in bunches and has a creamy flesh and a smooth skin. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","In particular, the sweet, yellow fruit of the Cavendish banana cultivar, which may be eaten raw, as distinct from e.g. a plantain for cooking."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Curved like a banana, especially of a ball in flight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/banana"},{"term":"bandanna","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of bandana."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bandanna"}],"entries":[{"title":"Banana","blocks":[{"text":"A banana is an elongated, edible fruit that is botanically a berry produced by several kinds of large treelike herbaceous flowering plants in the genus Musa. In some countries, cooking bananas are called plantains, distinguishing them from dessert bananas. The fruit is variable in size, color and firmness, but is usually elongated and curved, with soft flesh rich in starch covered with a peel, which may have a variety of colors when ripe. It grows upward in clusters near the top of the plant. Almost all modern edible seedless (parthenocarp) cultivated bananas come from two wild species – Musa acuminata and Musa balbisiana, or their hybrids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Bananavarieties.jpg/330px-Bananavarieties.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banana","wikidata":"Q503"},{"title":"Kerchief","blocks":[{"text":"A kerchief, also known as a bandana or bandanna, is a triangular or square piece of cloth tied around the head, face, or neck for protective or decorative purposes. The popularity of head kerchiefs may vary by culture or religion, often being used as a Christian headcovering by men and women of the Anabaptist, Eastern Orthodox, and Plymouth Brethren denominations, as well as by some Orthodox Jewish and Muslim men and women and is also considered a hat."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Ari%27s_back.jpg/330px-Ari%27s_back.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerchief","wikidata":"Q101444738"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Banana_corm.jpg/250px-Banana_corm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Banana_corm.jpg/330px-Banana_corm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Banana_corm.jpg/250px-Banana_corm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABanana_corm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Young_Banana_Sapling_-_Kerala_-_IMG_3447.jpg/120px-Young_Banana_Sapling_-_Kerala_-_IMG_3447.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Young_Banana_Sapling_-_Kerala_-_IMG_3447.jpg/250px-Young_Banana_Sapling_-_Kerala_-_IMG_3447.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Young_Banana_Sapling_-_Kerala_-_IMG_3447.jpg/120px-Young_Banana_Sapling_-_Kerala_-_IMG_3447.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoung_Banana_Sapling_-_Kerala_-_IMG_3447.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/M._acuminata_x_balbisiana_female_flower_detail.jpg/250px-M._a